<a href="#section-send-execution" id="section-send-execution" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
<h2>Choose how sends execute</h2>
<section>
  <div class="view">
    <p>If the next line of your job depends on the send finishing, call <code>mailer.sync().sendMail(email)</code>. It returns the submission
      receipt on the calling thread and throws failures there. If an HTTP request can return while SMTP work continues, call
      <code>mailer.async().sendMail(email)</code> and keep the returned <code>MailSend&lt;MailSubmissionReceipt&gt;</code>.</p>
    <p>Choose at each call. <code>sync()</code> and <code>async()</code> return cached views of the same Mailer and start no work on their own.
      Use the same views for <code>testConnection()</code> and <code>probeConnection()</code>.</p>
    <p>The async example records the outcome when it arrives. Every <code>getCompletion()</code> call gives you a fresh
      <code>CompletableFuture</code> view. Cancelling, completing, or setting <code>orTimeout(...)</code> on that future changes only
      that view. To ask the Mailer to stop the send itself, call <code>send.requestCancellation()</code>.</p>
    <p>An async call rejects a null email immediately. The executor selects the destination, applies its templates, validates the email,
      and reports those failures on the completion. It also opens a simple batch's iterable lazily. A direct or caller-runs executor can
      do that work on the caller's thread.</p>
    <p>Ordinary <code>buildEmail()</code> creates the submitted message. The selected destination combines its applicable defaults and overrides
      during preparation, using each field's documented precedence or merge rules. Use
      <code>buildEmailCompletedWithDefaultsAndOverrides(...)</code> when you explicitly need that completion earlier; see
      <a href="understanding-configuration.html#configuration-resolution-timing">when choices are combined</a>.</p>
    <p>To use another permitted server after a connection failure, configure <a href="/smtp-connection-failover.html">SMTP connection failover</a>.
      It shares this operation's queue, deadline, cancellation and observer. Busy pools and sending-limit waits do not trigger it.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="side"><pre><code class="language-java">MailSend&lt;MailSubmissionReceipt&gt; send =
    mailer.async().sendMail(email);

send.getCompletion().whenComplete((receipt, failure) -&gt; {
    if (failure == null) {
        recordSubmission(receipt);
    } else {
        recordFailure(failure);
    }
});

// Or wait, without losing the ability to request cancellation:
MailSubmissionReceipt receipt = send.getCompletion().join();</code></pre></div>
</section>
<a href="#section-international-mail" id="section-international-mail" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  {{#> components/feature-heading-h2 core=true}}International mail and older SMTP servers{{/components/feature-heading-h2}}

  <section>
      <div class="wide">
          <p>
              You can send <em>Welcome, José</em> to <code>jose@example.org</code> using ordinary MIME encoding. Sending that same
              message to <code>müller@example.org</code> needs SMTPUTF8 because the mailbox itself contains a non-ASCII character. A finalized
              message with raw UTF-8 headers or raw 8-bit body bytes can also need SMTP extensions.
          </p>
          <p>
              You can send to both addresses through one Mailer. On library-owned stock Angus Sessions, Simple Java Mail enables UTF-8
              encoding before preparing MIME or creating transports. It requests SMTPUTF8 only for submissions that need it, so both kinds
              of email can reuse the same connections.
          </p>
<pre><code class="language-java small">Mailer mailer = mail.mailerBuilder()
    .withSMTPServer("smtp.example.org", 587)
    .buildMailer();

Email email = mail.emailBuilder().startingBlank()
    .from("sender@example.org")
    .withRecipients(mail.recipientBuilder().withName("Müller").withAddress("müller@example.org").withType(Message.RecipientType.TO).build())
    .withPlainText("Hello!")
    .buildEmail();

mailer.sync().sendMail(email); // the receipt is optional to inspect</code></pre>
          <p>
              By default, the connected server must advertise SMTPUTF8 for internationalized envelope addresses or raw UTF-8 headers, and 8BITMIME for
              raw 8-bit body bytes. The adapter adds the appropriate parameters when needed. An <code class="inline language-text">8bit</code> label on an
              entirely ASCII body does not by itself require 8BITMIME. Existing capability-dependent Angus 8-bit conversion remains available.
          </p>
          <p>
              Use the <a href="debugging.html#section-smtp-capabilities">connection probe</a> to inspect a deployment. Sending still checks the actual
              connection, including its capabilities after STARTTLS. A probe is diagnostic; it does not upgrade a Mailer or certify the onward route.
          </p>
          <h3>Send the same message through three routes</h3>
          <p>Send the message above to <code>müller@example.org</code> through a library-owned Angus Session.</p>
          <dl class="extension-boundary-map">
            <div><dt>Relay advertises SMTPUTF8</dt><dd>The Mailer adds SMTPUTF8 when it submits the message.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Relay omits SMTPUTF8</dt><dd>With the default policy, the Mailer rejects the send before submission.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Verified legacy route</dt><dd>Explicitly allow original content even without the advertisement.
              The Mailer attempts the same address without adding an unadvertised SMTPUTF8 parameter.</dd></div>
          </dl>
          <p>Enable the option only after verifying the legacy relay and the onward route. The relay can still refuse the message.</p>
<pre><code class="language-java small">Mailer mailer = mail.mailerBuilder()
    .withSMTPServer("legacy-relay.example.org", 587)
    .withConnectionConfig(ConnectionConfig.builder()
        .withLegacySmtpContentSupport(true)
        .build())
    .buildMailer();</code></pre>
<pre><code class="language-properties small">simplejavamail.smtp.legacycontentsupport=true</code></pre>
<details class="doc-disclosure">
  <summary>What the legacy route still checks</summary>
<ul>
            <li><strong>What the Mailer will attempt.</strong> The option covers internationalized addresses, raw UTF-8 headers and raw 8-bit bodies. Advertised extensions are still used normally.
              For a missing extension, the adapter attempts the original content without automatically adding the unadvertised SMTPUTF8 or
              <code class="inline language-text">BODY=8BITMIME</code> parameter. Explicit advanced provider parameters remain your responsibility.
              A genuine server rejection is reported normally; there is no automatic retry with changed content or fewer requirements.</li>
            <li><strong>What acceptance tells you.</strong> This deliberately operates outside the negotiated guarantees in
              <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6531.html#section-3.2">SMTPUTF8</a> and
              <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6152.html#section-3">8BITMIME</a>.
              An accepted <code class="inline language-java">MailSubmissionReceipt</code> confirms SMTP submission, not downstream preservation or final delivery.
              Enabling the option does not make a server that rejects internationalized addresses accept them.</li>
            <li><strong>What the Mailer still checks.</strong> No address characters are replaced, and exact or signed content is never rewritten. TLS, REQUIRETLS and explicit DSN requirements remain
              enforced. Internationalized ORCPT metadata still requires advertised SMTPUTF8 support. Malformed headers, binary transfer encoding,
              NUL body bytes and body lines longer than 998 bytes (excluding CRLF) remain rejected; encode unsuitable bodies as Base64 or quoted-printable.</li>
          </ul>

          <p>
              An explicit <code class="inline language-properties">mail.mime.allowutf8=false</code> is honored. Caller-owned Sessions, CustomMailer and
              third-party providers keep their existing ownership. For a caller-owned ordinary Angus transport, enable
              <code class="inline language-properties">mail.mime.allowutf8=true</code> before creating transports if needed; Angus retains its Session-wide
              SMTPUTF8 declaration behavior there, including on ASCII messages. Custom socket factories on library-owned stock Angus Sessions are preserved
              and still receive managed per-message negotiation; they do not automatically gain physical-abort support.
          </p>
          <p>
              The compatibility permission belongs to the configuration of the Session actually selected for sending. In a shared cluster, enabling it
              for one server does not enable it for the others. See <a href="configuration.html#section-legacy-smtp-content">configuration</a>,
              <a href="spring.html#section-spring-profiles">Spring profiles</a> and <a href="cli.html#section-legacy-smtp-content">CLI usage</a>.
              Angus may log that SMTPUTF8 is unavailable; that notice alone does not prevent ASCII-compatible mail.
          </p>
</details>
      </div>
  </section>

<a href="#section-send-architecture" id="section-send-architecture" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
<h2>Inside a mail send</h2>
<section>
  <p class="wide">These are the pieces behind the configuration on this page. The executor controls waiting work, the pool manages reusable
    connections, and the transport talks SMTP. Outcome reporting follows each email across those layers; it is separate from the work of sending it.</p>
  {{> components/mail-send-architecture}}
  <p class="wide">This is a responsibility map, not a lock-order diagram. Direct sends, shared batches, custom mailers, and caller-owned Sessions
    have different paths. Use the sections below for their execution and ownership rules.</p>
</section>

  <a href="#section-async-queue" id="section-async-queue" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  {{#> components/feature-heading-h2 core=true}}Bound asynchronous work and handle a full queue{{/components/feature-heading-h2}}

  <section>
    <div class="view">
      <p>
        Your nightly report job can create 500 emails long before the SMTP server catches up. A worker limit controls how many tasks are
        running. Add a queue limit to control how many get to wait. When the queue fills, your job can slow down, put the reports back on
        its durable queue, or skip a notification that has gone stale.
      </p>
      <p>
        The example allows four workers and 100 waiting tasks per Mailer. The default <code>-1</code> leaves that queue unbounded.
        Set <code>0</code> when a send should be accepted only if a worker can take it immediately. Capacity belongs to the Mailer,
        regardless of which SMTP connections or clusters its workers use. A full built-in queue never runs SMTP on the caller as a fallback.
      </p>
      <p>
        The next task is rejected immediately once all four workers and 100 waiting slots are occupied. Its future fails with
        <code>MailSendRejectedException</code>, a <code>MailException</code> with kind <code>CAPACITY</code>. An ordinary email's
        observer is called once on the submitting thread with no receipt or started timestamp. Rejected tasks never acquire a transport or proxy.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="side">
<pre><code class="language-java small">Mailer mailer = mail.mailerBuilder()
    .withSMTPServer("smtp.example.org", 587, "user", "password")
    .withTransportStrategy(TransportStrategy.SMTP_TLS)
    .withAsyncExecutionConfig(AsyncExecutionConfig.builder()
        .withThreadPoolSize(4) // explicit worker count also applies without the batch module
        .withQueueCapacity(100)
        .rejectWhenFull()
        .build())
    .buildMailer();

mailer.async().sendMail(email).getCompletion().whenComplete((ignored, failure) -&gt; {
    Throwable cause = failure instanceof CompletionException ? failure.getCause() : failure;
    if (cause instanceof MailSendRejectedException) {
        MailSendRejectedException rejected = (MailSendRejectedException) cause;
        logger.warn("Mail was not scheduled: {}", rejected.getReason());
    } else if (cause != null) {
        logger.warn("Mail send failed", cause);
    }
});</code></pre>
      <p>
        Rejection means this attempt was not scheduled. Other send failures can represent an accepted or ambiguous SMTP transaction:
        inspect the <a href="/analyzing-send-results.html#section-get-receipt">submission receipt</a> before deciding to retry.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="view">
      <h4>Let a background producer wait briefly</h4>
      <p>
        If the report job can slow down, let it wait up to 250 ms for a slot. <code>waitForCapacity(Duration)</code> blocks the
        submitting thread, so keep it off request threads and event loops that need to return promptly. An observer that submits work
        can wait as well. If that observer is on the only worker and the queue is full, the timeout may be what lets the worker continue.
      </p>
      <p>
        Look for <code>CAPACITY_WAIT_TIMED_OUT</code>, <code>CAPACITY_WAIT_INTERRUPTED</code>, or <code>EXECUTOR_SHUT_DOWN</code>
        when a wait fails, and <code>QUEUE_FULL</code> for immediate rejection. An interrupted wait preserves the thread's interrupt flag.
        For a borrowed executor, <code>EXECUTOR_REJECTED</code> retains its original <code>RejectedExecutionException</code> as the cause.
        The admission timeout applies only while submitting the task.
      </p>
<pre><code class="language-java small">mailerBuilder.withAsyncExecutionConfig(AsyncExecutionConfig.builder()
    .withQueueCapacity(100)
    .waitForCapacity(Duration.ofMillis(250))
    .build());</code></pre>
    </div>
    <div class="side">
      <h4>Know what the bound counts</h4>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Active workers.</strong> A worker still counts as active while it prepares a destination-specific Email or waits for a connection. Waiting tasks start from a
          FIFO queue and can finish in any order when workers run together. The queue limit counts tasks after you submit them, so it cannot
          cap the memory your code has already used to build messages and attachments.</li>
        <li><strong>Tasks in the queue.</strong> An async simple batch occupies one slot and opens its iterable once it runs. A rejected batch leaves the iterator untouched and
          reports no per-email outcomes. Asynchronous connection tests join the same queue. Synchronous and open-connection operations bypass it.</li>
        <li><strong>A supplied executor.</strong> To use your own queue and rejection policy, pass
          <code>withAsyncExecutionConfig(AsyncExecutionConfig.builder().withExecutorService(executor).build())</code>. A custom executor
          replaces explicit built-in queue settings and leaves shutdown to you. These settings apply only to the Mailer. A standalone
          <code>BatchTransportExecutor</code> has its own executor.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Use <a href="debugging.html#section-async-queue">queue snapshots</a> for counts and rejection reasons, and
        <a href="/analyzing-send-results.html#section-send-timings">actual-send measurements</a> for individual scheduling delay. For lifecycle behavior, see
        <a href="#section-mailer-lifecycle">graceful shutdown</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>


<a href="#section-sending-limits" id="section-sending-limits" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
{{#> components/feature-heading-h2 core=true}}Match your SMTP service's sending limits{{/components/feature-heading-h2}}
{{> components/pattern-examples topic="sending-limits"}}
<section>
  <div class="view">
    <p>Your relay plan allows 30 messages and 100 recipients per minute, and several invoice workers can send at once.
      Put the rules on the Mailer so each worker uses the same allowance. They apply to synchronous and asynchronous sends, simple batches,
      and <code>withOpenConnection(...)</code>.</p>
    <p>Use the counts and periods from your provider. Sending limits default to off. With both rules enabled, an invoice needs
      room in both rolling windows, measured from recent attempts instead of the start of the clock minute.</p>
    <p>Connection-pool size still controls concurrent connections, and queue capacity still controls waiting tasks. Neither is a sending rate.
      These limits do not regulate bandwidth or coordinate your service account across applications.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="side">
<pre><code class="language-java small">SimpleJavaMail mail = SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults();

Mailer mailer = mail.mailerBuilder()
    .withSMTPServer("smtp.example.org", 587, "user", "password")
    .withTransportStrategy(TransportStrategy.SMTP_TLS)
    .withSendingLimitsConfig(SendingLimitsConfig.builder()
        .withMessageRateLimit(30, Duration.ofMinutes(1))
        .withRecipientRateLimit(100, Duration.ofMinutes(1))
        .withRateLimitGroup("company-account")
        .withRateLimitBurstsAllowed(false)
        .build())
    .buildMailer();

// The same send API; the caller or worker waits when needed.
mailer.async().sendMail(email);</code></pre>
    <p>On <code>SendingLimitsConfig.Builder</code>, calling a setter again replaces that rule. Use <code>resetMessageRateLimit()</code> or <code>resetRecipientRateLimit()</code>
      to remove it. <code>resetRateLimitGroup()</code> restores private allowance, while <code>resetRateLimitBurstsAllowed()</code>
      restores the default of allowing bursts. Supply the resulting value through <code>withSendingLimitsConfig(...)</code>.
      These resets affect the next Mailer you build, not an existing Mailer.</p>
  </div>
</section>
<section>
  <div class="view">
    <h3>Allow a burst, or spread sends out</h3>
    <p>Say 30 invoices are ready at the top of the hour. By default, their message rule can let all 30 start immediately. Further
      submissions wait for earlier attempts to leave the rolling window. Set <code>withRateLimitBurstsAllowed(false)</code> to spread those starts out.</p>
    <p>With spreading, 30 messages per minute means at least two seconds between local submission starts; 120 per minute means half a second.
      The first can start immediately. A slow submission does not add another full interval after it finishes, and submissions may overlap.
      Idle time does not build up catch-up credit.</p>
    <p>Recipients have a weight: sending to ten recipients against a 100-per-minute rule puts at least six seconds before the following
      submission starts. The rolling-window ceiling still applies. An email with more recipients than the whole configured allowance is
      rejected clearly, not split up or left waiting forever.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="side">
    <h3>Use properties when the deployment chooses the rate</h3>
<pre><code class="language-properties small">simplejavamail.smtp.ratelimit.group=company-account
simplejavamail.smtp.ratelimit.messages.limit=30
simplejavamail.smtp.ratelimit.messages.period=PT1M
simplejavamail.smtp.ratelimit.recipients.limit=100
simplejavamail.smtp.ratelimit.recipients.period=PT1M
simplejavamail.smtp.ratelimit.allowbursts=false</code></pre>
    <p>Each rule needs a positive count and a positive ISO-8601 duration, such as <code>PT1M</code> or <code>PT0.5S</code>.
      Remove both the count and period to leave that rule disabled. See <a href="configuration.html#section-config-properties">configuration precedence</a>,
      <a href="spring.html#section-spring-sending-limits">Spring configuration</a>, and <a href="cli.html#section-sending-limits">CLI usage</a>.</p>
  </div>
</section>
<section>
  <div class="wide">
    <h3>Share one service account's allowance</h3>
    <p>If Billing and Notifications use the same relay account, build their Mailers from the same application-scoped
      <code>SimpleJavaMail</code> factory. Give both the same <code>withRateLimitGroup("company-account")</code> and matching message,
      recipient, and burst settings. They draw from one allowance even with separate connection pools. A mismatch fails the build.</p>

    <h3>What counts against the limit?</h3>
    <p>Say the two Mailers share a limit of <strong>3 submission attempts and 8 recipients per minute</strong>. Here is what stays
      available as they send within the same rolling window.</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Billing sends an invoice to one customer and BCCs the archive.</strong> That uses one attempt and two recipients.
        <strong>2 attempts and 6 recipients left.</strong></li>
      <li><strong>Notifications sends to one person and gets a temporary <code>451</code> refusal for the whole message.</strong> The submission still counts.
        <strong>1 attempt and 5 recipients left.</strong></li>
      <li><strong>Notifications retries under its configured <a href="/mail-send-retries.html">retry policy</a>.</strong> This uses one more
        attempt and recipient. <strong>0 attempts and 4 recipients left.</strong> Another send waits for room in both limits.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>In a separate send with allowance available, suppose Billing cannot connect at all. If the send stops before invoking the
      provider, the Mailer releases its reservation. The connection failure uses no allowance.</p>

    <details class="doc-disclosure">
      <summary>How the Mailer counts attempts and recipients</summary>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Provider invocation.</strong> Ordinary sends reserve allowance before acquiring a connection or starting a proxy.
          Cancellation, connection failure, or MIME-preparation failure releases the reservation before the provider call. Once invoked,
          it counts even if provider preflight fails, the SMTP reply is a refusal, acceptance is unknown, or cleanup fails.
          The rate measures local invocation starts. Arrival times can differ.</li>
        <li><strong>SMTP envelope.</strong> The Mailer counts each intended envelope recipient occurrence, including BCC, additional recipient
          headers, and explicit envelope overrides. Repeated occurrences and rejected recipients count.</li>
        <li><strong>Other operations.</strong> A <a href="/configuration.html#section-custom-mailer">CustomMailer</a> invocation counts once with its
          intended envelope. Extra sends or retries inside that callback are your responsibility. Validation, rehearsal, connection tests,
          probes, and logging-only sends use no allowance.</li>
      </ul>
    </details>

    <details class="doc-disclosure">
      <summary>Groups, clusters, and separate processes</summary>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Private allowance.</strong> Each registered SMTP configuration without a group name has its own allowance.
          A group name is independent of pool clusters and hostnames. In a cluster, the configuration selected for each send supplies
          its limits, even when you called a different Mailer.</li>
        <li><strong>History.</strong> Closing and rebuilding a Mailer keeps its named group's recent history. A failed build leaves group rules
          unset if they were never established. Other factories, JVMs, containers, and CLI processes keep independent histories. Apply any
          daily, persistent, or service-account-wide quota outside this in-process limiter.</li>
        <li><strong>Supplied Sessions.</strong> Reusing a caller-owned Session leaves each Mailer's limits in place. Within a single cluster,
          that Session identifies one pool. Reuse the Mailer or its factory's group, or supply a separate Session for a separately
          configured participant.</li>
      </ul>
    </details>

    <details class="doc-disclosure">
      <summary>Rate waits, connections, and shutdown</summary>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Workers.</strong> The caller or worker waits until there is allowance, cancellation, or a send deadline. There is
          no separate rate-wait timeout. Waiters are FIFO, so an email with many recipients can hold up smaller ones. Bound the async
          queue if the producers can outrun the rate. More workers still share the same allowance.</li>
        <li><strong>Connections.</strong> Ordinary sends wait before borrowing a connection. A simple batch or <code>withOpenConnection(...)</code>
          holds its connection while waiting between emails. That can exceed the server's idle timeout. The Mailer keeps the limit and
          does not reconnect automatically. Prefer ordinary pooled sends if you expect long waits.</li>
        <li><strong>Shutdown.</strong> Graceful shutdown waits for accepted rate-limited work. Keep async handles to request cancellation
          or set a deadline if you need to bound the wait.</li>
      </ul>
    </details>
  </div>
  <div class="wide full-width-example">
    <p>Read the resolved rules through <code>mailer.getOperationalConfig()</code> and trace their sources in
      <a href="debugging.html#section-config-diagnostics">configuration diagnostics</a>. If a billing run takes longer than expected,
      record how long each send waited for allowance with
      <a href="analyzing-send-results.html#section-send-timings"><code>MailSendDiagnostics.getRateLimitWait()</code></a>.</p>
<pre><code class="language-java small">mailerBuilder.withSendHandlers(MailSendHandlers.builder()
    .withResultObserver(outcome -> outcome.getDiagnostics().ifPresent(diagnostics -> {
        Duration wait = diagnostics.getRateLimitWait().getElapsed();
        if (wait != null) {
            metrics.recordRateWait(wait);
        }
    }))
    .build());</code></pre>
  </div>
</section>

<a href="#section-send-deadlines" id="section-send-deadlines" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
<h2>Deadlines and cancellation</h2>
<section>
  <div class="wide full-width-example">
    <p>Suppose a sign-in code is only useful while a customer is at the login screen. Give the send 30 seconds, including any time it
      spends waiting to run. If the customer requests a new code sooner, use the old send's handle to ask the Mailer to stop it.</p>
<pre><code class="language-java">Mailer mailer = mailerBuilder
    .withMailSendTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(30))
    .buildMailer();

MailSend&lt;MailSubmissionReceipt&gt; send = mailer.async().sendMail(email);
send.getCompletion().whenComplete(this::recordCompletion);

// Later, if the customer requests a replacement code:
send.requestCancellation();</code></pre>
  </div>
  <div class="wide">
    <h3>Check how far the send got</h3>
    <p><code>requestCancellation()</code> is safe to call again. The completion supplies the result after the work and required cleanup finish.
      A <code>MailSendCancelledException</code> or <code>MailSendTimeoutException</code> can carry an SMTP receipt.
      <a href="/analyzing-send-results.html#section-control-results">Check the available submission evidence before retrying.</a></p>
    <div class="doc-table-wrap" tabindex="0" aria-label="What cancellation means at each stage of a send"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>When it stops</th><th>What to expect</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Queued</td><td>A task that stops before it runs never reaches SMTP. The Mailer's own queue frees the slot.
          A supplied executor may leave the cancelled task queued, where it can no longer send.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Connecting</td><td>Supported connections can abort setup, including TLS, authentication, and supported proxies.
          A send can stop before it has a submission receipt.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Uploading</td><td>If the provider can establish the final DATA terminator or BDAT LAST was never sent, the receipt can
          show that the server could not have accepted the message.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Awaiting the final reply</td><td>If the terminator could have reached the server and the reply is lost, expect
          <code>UNKNOWN</code> with <code>DUPLICATE_RISK</code>. Acceptance may already have happened.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>After acceptance</td><td>A send can still succeed when acceptance is already known.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
    <p id="late-stop-cleanup">If unrelated cleanup fails after acceptance, the failure still carries its original cause and any accepted
      receipt. The provider handles the expected socket-close errors from physically aborting the connection.</p>

    <details class="doc-disclosure">
      <summary>Configure the deadline and related timeouts</summary>
      <p>The total deadline is off by default. Set a positive <code>Duration</code> that fits in nanoseconds or use an ISO-8601 value
        in configuration. <code>resetMailSendTimeout()</code> removes the configured deadline.</p>
<pre><code class="language-properties">simplejavamail.defaults.mailsend.timeout=PT30S</code></pre>
      <p>The same property works in <a href="/spring.html">Spring</a>. In the CLI, use <code>--mailer:withMailSendTimeout PT30S</code>.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Whole send.</strong> <code>withMailSendTimeout(...)</code> covers admission, preparation, pool acquisition, connection
          setup, TLS, authentication, MIME and security work, SMTP, and required cleanup. Completion can take longer when work must return
          cooperatively.</li>
        <li><strong>Socket operations.</strong> Shorter connect, read, and write limits still apply.
          <code>ConnectionConfig.Builder.withSessionTimeout(...)</code> sets all three. Use <code>withConnectTimeout(...)</code>,
          <code>withReadTimeout(...)</code>, and <code>withWriteTimeout(...)</code> to tune them individually.
          See <a href="configuration.html#socket-timeouts">fine-tuning and precedence</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>Waiting for capacity.</strong> <code>AsyncExecutionConfig.Builder.waitForCapacity(...)</code> bounds waiting for
          admission on the submitting thread. <code>ConnectionPoolConfig.Builder.withClaimTimeout(...)</code> bounds waiting for a lease.</li>
      </ul>
    </details>

    <details class="doc-disclosure">
      <summary>Providers, clusters, and write timers</summary>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Provider support.</strong> A total deadline requires a supported physical abort. The Mailer fails before connecting
          if the selected provider, socket factory, <code>CustomMailer</code>, or caller-owned Session lacks it. Keep the deadline off
          there to use cooperative cancellation. Your supplied providers and socket factories stay in place.</li>
        <li><strong>Cluster support.</strong> Every member must support physical abort for timed sends. After a timed send, the cluster
          also checks new registrations until its last Session is removed.</li>
        <li><strong>Aborting a pooled socket.</strong> Managed Angus can close the socket without waiting for the SMTP monitor. Aborting
          a pooled connection invalidates and disposes of the lease, so a later borrower cannot inherit the cancellation.</li>
        <li><strong>Write timers.</strong> Physical connections on a managed Angus Session share one lazy daemon timer thread. Returning
          a lease or aborting its raw socket leaves the connection's timer reference in place until disposal. The last disposal stops the
          thread. A later connection can start it again. The application owns any scheduler it supplies.
          See <a href="configuration.html#section-programmatic-api-other">configuration options</a>.</li>
      </ul>
    </details>

    <details class="doc-disclosure">
      <summary>Callbacks, batches, and open connections</summary>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Cooperative cleanup.</strong> DNS, application callbacks, custom data sources, and provider code outside supported
          I/O must return on their own. Observer work stays outside the budget. An inline observer can delay completion, but deadline
          signalling runs independently of observers and <code>CompletableFuture</code> continuations.</li>
        <li><strong>Simple batches.</strong> One budget covers the iteration, excluding observer work between emails. On failure or
          a known stop, the Mailer stops before its next source callback. A callback already running must return on its own. It checks
          again after each inline observer, including the final one, before calling <code>hasNext()</code>. Successful outcomes stay
          successful and untouched emails receive no callbacks. Rejection or cancellation before execution leaves the iterable unopened.</li>
        <li><strong>Open connections.</strong> <code>withOpenConnection(...)</code> gives connection setup and each send its own budget.
          Application work between sends and the final scope cleanup are outside those per-send budgets.</li>
        <li><strong>Other operations.</strong> Validation, rehearsal, connection tests, and standalone <code>BatchTransportExecutor</code>
          work are outside the Mailer send deadline and cancellation API.</li>
      </ul>
    </details>
  </div>
</section>

<a href="#section-batch-and-clustering" id="section-batch-and-clustering" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
<h2>Connection reuse and clusters</h2>
<section><p class="wide">Queue limits count waiting tasks; pool limits count reusable connections. A worker waiting for a lease is still active.
  Use the direct, simple-batch, open-connection, or pooled path that matches your workload.</p></section>
  <a href="#section-not-reusing-connections" id="section-not-reusing-connections" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
  {{#> components/feature-heading-h3 core=true}}Send one email on a direct connection{{/components/feature-heading-h3}}
  <section><div class="wide">
    <p>For an occasional password reset, let the Mailer open a transport, send the message and close the transport.
      With your SMTP settings already on <code>mailerBuilder</code>, a one-off job can own the Mailer for the duration of the call.</p>
<pre><code class="language-java">try (Mailer mailer = mailerBuilder.buildMailer()) {
    mailer.sync().sendMail(email);
}</code></pre>
    <p>A service can reuse the Mailer for later calls. Direct async sending uses one worker by default. If you
      <a href="/configuration.html#configure-async-workers">supply more workers</a>, allow one connection per simultaneous send.
      Choose <code>sync()</code> or <code>async()</code> at the call site and handle the result there.
      See <a href="#section-mailer-lifecycle">when to close the Mailer and executor</a>.</p>
  </div></section>

  <h3 id="section-simple-batch">Send a known set in a simple batch</h3>
  <section><div class="wide">
    <p>For an overnight set of reports, let the Mailer read the iterable lazily and send one report at a time on a shared connection.
      The batch stops at the first failure.</p>
<pre><code class="language-java">mailer.sync().sendMailsInSimpleBatch(emails);</code></pre>
    <p>For a background batch use <code>mailer.async().sendMailsInSimpleBatch(emails)</code> and check the returned
      <code>MailSend&lt;Void&gt;</code> completion. The sync call returns <code>void</code>. For individual results, register a
      <a href="#section-mail-send-observer">result observer</a>. If you need to checkpoint each receipt yourself, use the callback below.</p>
<details class="doc-disclosure">
  <summary>Iteration, cleanup and partial batch results</summary>
<p>The execution thread opens and reads the iterable. Observer handoff and timing follow the ordinary send rules.
      Queue admission, iteration, connection setup and final cleanup can fail outside an individual send. Watch the batch completion
      as well as outcomes for the messages reached. Keep the job ID with each outcome when deciding what is safe to retry.</p>
      <p>If preparing an acquired connection fails, the transport is closed. A subsequent close failure is suppressed on the original failure.
      For sustained throughput, see <a href="#section-reusing-connections">pooled connections</a>.</p>
</details>
  </div></section>

  <h3 id="section-open-connection">Checkpoint each send on an open connection</h3>
  <section><div class="wide">
    <p>When the queue belongs to your application, use <code>withOpenConnection(...)</code> to inspect each receipt and checkpoint
      the job before reading the next item. Use the scoped sender on the callback’s thread while the callback is running.</p>
<pre><code class="language-java">mailer.withOpenConnection(sender -&gt; {
    while (database.hasPendingMail()) {
        Email next = database.nextPendingMail();
        MailSubmissionReceipt receipt = sender.sendMail(next);
        database.recordSubmission(next, receipt);
    }
});</code></pre>
    <p>A failed or partially accepted submission throws <code>MailSubmissionException</code>. Inspect its receipt when available before deciding
      what to record or retry. Calls on the scoped sender from another thread or after the callback ends fail before preparing an email.</p>
<details class="doc-disclosure">
  <summary>Connection ownership and failover</summary>
<p>Normally the Mailer opens a dedicated connection and closes it after the callback. With
      <a href="/smtp-connection-failover.html#section-batches">failover</a> and the batch module, it can return a healthy lease to the selected pool.
      You hold that lease while checkpointing. If an independent send needs the same full pool, schedule it after the callback returns.</p>
      <p>For independent background sends use <code>mailer.async().sendMail(...)</code>. For concurrent sending or cluster routing see
      <a href="#section-reusing-connections">pooled connections</a>. A <a href="/configuration.html#section-custom-mailer">CustomMailer</a>
      manages its own connections and uses the regular sync and async send APIs.</p>
</details>
  </div></section>

  <a href="#section-reusing-connections" id="section-reusing-connections" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
  {{#> components/feature-heading-h3 batch=true}}Reusing connections with a pool{{/components/feature-heading-h3}}

	<section>
		<div>
			<p class="wide">
				If receipts arrive in bursts throughout the day, a connection pool lets the next worker reuse a Transport the last send just
				returned. The default pool grows to four connections and closes idle connections five seconds after their last use.
			</p>
			<p class="wide">
				Start with the number of connections your relay and workers can use. Configure the core and maximum pool size, how long a worker
				can wait for a connection, and how long to keep it after a send.
			</p>
			<p class="wide">
				This section configures the Simple Java Mail <code>Mailer</code> path. Compare it with the standalone batch facade, direct leases, and
				provider-owned pooling in the <a href="smtp-connection-pooling.html">pool orchestration guide</a>.
			</p>
			<p>
				Even a small worker pool can benefit if connection setup takes a significant part of each send.
			</p>
		<div>
		<pre><code class="language-java small">Mailer pooledMailer = mailerBuilder
	   .(..)
	   .withConnectionPoolConfig(ConnectionPoolConfig.builder()
	       .withCoreSize(2) // keep 2 connections up at all times, automatically refreshed after expiry policy closes it (default 0)
	       .withMaxSize(10) // scale up to max 10 connections until expiry policy kicks in and cleans up (default 4)
	       .withClaimTimeout(Duration.ofMinutes(1)) // wait max 1 minute for available connection (default about 24.9 days)
	       .withExpireAfter(Duration.ofMinutes(30)) // keep connections spinning for half an hour (default 5 seconds)
	       .build())
	   .buildMailer();
	</code></pre>
				<p class="wide">
					Or using properties:
				</p>
				<pre><code class="language-properties small">simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.coresize=2
simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.maxsize=10
simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.claimtimeout.millis=60000
simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.expireafter.millis=1800000
	</code></pre>
		  <p class="wide">
			  A live connection pool keeps the JVM running. See <a href="#section-mailer-lifecycle">Mailer lifecycle and resource ownership</a> for orderly
          application shutdown, including clustered Mailers.
		  </p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>


	<a href="#section-clustering" id="section-clustering" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
	{{#> components/feature-heading-h3 batch=true}}Clustered SMTP load balancing{{/components/feature-heading-h3}}

	<section>
		<div>
			<div class="view">
				<p>
					Suppose two order relays handle the same receipts, while account alerts use a separate relay. Build an order Mailer for each
					relay with the same cluster key. Each Mailer registers its Session's pool. Give alerts a separate key to keep their pool separate.
				</p>
				<p>
					The cluster picks a registered pool for each send using round-robin or random-access selection.
				</p>
				<p>
					For cluster-selected versus exact-Session callbacks outside the full Mailer API, see the
					<a href="smtp-connection-pooling.html#option-batch-facade">standalone batch-module facade</a>.
				</p>
			</div>

			<div class="side">
				Cluster-level Java config:
				<pre><code>UUID ordersCluster = UUID.fromString("00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000301");

Mailer clusteredMailer = mailerBuilder
    .(..) // normal SMTP settings
    .withConnectionPoolConfig(ConnectionPoolConfig.builder()
        .withClusterKey(ordersCluster)
        .withCoreSize(2)
        .withMaxSize(10)
        .withClaimTimeout(Duration.ofMinutes(1))
        .withExpireAfter(Duration.ofMinutes(30))
        .withLoadBalancingStrategy(LoadBalancingStrategy.ROUND_ROBIN)
        .build())
    .buildMailer();</code></pre>
				or using properties:
				<pre><code class="language-properties small">simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.clusterkey.uuid=00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000301
simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.loadbalancing.strategy=ROUND_ROBIN
# valid values: ROUND_ROBIN, RANDOM_ACCESS</code></pre>
			</div>

			<div class="wide full-width-example">
				<p><strong>Try one send through any registered orders relay.</strong></p>
				<pre><code class="language-java small">SimpleJavaMail mail = SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults();

Mailer primary = mail.mailerBuilder()
    .withSMTPServer("smtp-primary.example.com", 587, "user", "password")
    .withConnectionPoolConfig(ConnectionPoolConfig.builder()
        .withClusterKey(myClusterKey)
        .build())
    .buildMailer();
Mailer secondary = mail.mailerBuilder()
    .withSMTPServer("smtp-secondary.example.com", 587, "user", "password")
    .withConnectionPoolConfig(ConnectionPoolConfig.builder()
        .withClusterKey(myClusterKey)
        .build())
    .buildMailer();
Mailer tertiary = mail.mailerBuilder()
    .withSMTPServer("smtp-tertiary.example.com", 587, "user", "password")
    .withConnectionPoolConfig(ConnectionPoolConfig.builder()
        .withClusterKey(myClusterKey)
        .build())
    .buildMailer();

try {
    primary.sync().sendMail(email); // one registered pool is selected for this send
} catch (MailException failure) {
    // The selected SMTP submission failed.
    // Its connection is discarded, but its server pool stays in the cluster.
    // Apply your retry, provider-routing, or circuit-breaking policy here.
}</code></pre>
			</div>
		</div>

		<p class="wide">
			If a transport fails, the Mailer invalidates it and reports the failure. The same pool stays registered and can be selected for
			later sends. A synchronous call throws <code class="inline language-java">MailException</code>. In the async case the
			<a href="/analyzing-send-results.html#section-async-results"><em>CompletableFuture</em> completes exceptionally</a>.
			Check the result before retrying, especially if SMTP acceptance is uncertain. Use the per-send failure signal in any health-check,
			circuit-breaking, or provider-routing policy you own.
		</p>

	  <p class="wide">
		  <strong>Decide pool defaults before registering the first Mailer.</strong> The first registration sets the defaults for the cluster.
		  Later Mailers with that key inherit them. Separate cluster keys can have different defaults.
	  </p>

	  <p class="wide">
		  <strong>Configure several clusters when routes need different defaults.</strong> The Java API accepts any number of cluster keys.
		  <code class="inline language-properties">simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.clusterkey.uuid</code> defines one default key.
		  Use <code class="inline language-properties">simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.clusters.*</code> to define different defaults by
		  key in property files or <a href="/spring.html#section-spring-profiles">Spring</a>.
	  </p>

		<div class="wide">
			<p><strong>Property-defined cluster configs:</strong> Use one alias per cluster UUID. A named alias and a direct UUID alias may
              describe different clusters, but two aliases resolving to the same UUID are rejected during configuration loading.
              Keep all settings for that cluster under the chosen alias, including locked settings.</p>
			<pre><code class="language-properties small"># Alias-based config. The alias is only a property-file name; clusterkey.uuid is the actual cluster key.
simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.clusters.orders.clusterkey.uuid=00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000301
simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.clusters.orders.coresize=0
simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.clusters.orders.maxsize=3
simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.clusters.orders.claimtimeout.millis=30000
simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.clusters.orders.expireafter.millis=600000
simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.clusters.orders.loadbalancing.strategy=RANDOM_ACCESS

# Direct UUID-keyed config. If clusterkey.uuid is omitted, the alias itself must be a UUID.
simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.clusters.00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000302.maxsize=8
simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.clusters.00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000302.loadbalancing.strategy=ROUND_ROBIN
</code></pre>
		</div>

		<p class="wide">Choose worker, queue, and connection limits together with your SMTP service's connection and rate limits.
          More connections are useful only while the server and your workload can use them.</p>
	</section>
  <a href="#section-mail-send-observer" id="section-mail-send-observer" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  {{#> components/feature-heading-h2 core=true}}Observing completed mail sends{{/components/feature-heading-h2}}

  <section>
    <div class="view">
      <p>
        Suppose your billing service needs to record how every email finished, whether it was sent directly or in a batch.
        Register a <code class="inline language-java">MailSendResultObserver</code> on the reusable Mailer. The Mailer calls
        <code class="inline language-java">onMailSendCompleted(...)</code> for each reached email. A new registration replaces the old one.
      </p>
      <p>
        Use the <code class="inline language-java">MailSendOutcome</code> for the whole attempt, from preparation and queueing through
        completion. If you also need SMTP submission facts, read the optional <code class="inline language-java">MailSubmissionReceipt</code>.
        Preparation or queueing failures may have no receipt. A
        <code class="inline language-java">MailSubmissionException</code> carries the same receipt you get on the outcome.
      </p>
      <p>
        Use this callback to tally finished attempts. For live queueing, connecting, or lease events, see the live-progress observer below.
        The result callback receives outcome data without access to message content or SMTP resources.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="side">
<pre><code class="language-java small">Mailer mailer = mail.mailerBuilder()
    .withSMTPServer(
        "smtp.example.org", 587,
        "user", "password")
    .withSendHandlers(MailSendHandlers.builder().withResultObserver(
        this::recordMailSendOutcome).build())
    .buildMailer();

private void recordMailSendOutcome(
        MailSendOutcome outcome) {
    Duration totalTime = Duration.between(
        outcome.getRequestedAt(),
        outcome.getCompletedAt());

    audit.record(
        outcome.getEffectiveMessageId(),
        outcome.isSuccessful(),
        totalTime);

    outcome.getSubmissionReceipt()
        .ifPresent(receipt -> metrics
            .recordSubmissionStatus(
                receipt.getStatus()));

    outcome.getFailure()
        .ifPresent(failure -> metrics
            .recordFailure(failure.getClass()));
}</code></pre>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wide full-width-example">
      <p>
        Use a named observer if several Mailers should record the same metrics. This one counts successes, tracks logging-only runs,
        and measures elapsed time for attempts that started.
      </p>
  <pre><code class="language-java small">final class MetricsMailSendObserver implements MailSendResultObserver {

    @Override
    public void onMailSendCompleted(MailSendOutcome outcome) {
        Instant startedAt = outcome.getStartedAt();
        Duration sendTime = startedAt == null ? null
            : Duration.between(startedAt, outcome.getCompletedAt());

        metrics.recordMailSend(
            outcome.isSuccessful(),
            outcome.isLoggingOnly(),
            sendTime);
    }
}

Mailer mailer = mail.mailerBuilder()
    .withSendHandlers(MailSendHandlers.builder().withResultObserver(new MetricsMailSendObserver()).build())
    .buildMailer();</code></pre>
      <h3>Read the timestamps for one send</h3>
<pre><code class="language-text">requestedAt  → Begin the attempt
readyAt      → Ready for execution admission
startedAt    → Start executing
completedAt  → Record the terminal result</code></pre>

      <p>If scheduling fails, you can have <code>readyAt</code> without <code>startedAt</code>. Record the outcome and leave the duration unmeasured.</p>
<details class="doc-disclosure">
  <summary>Timing measurements, receipt fields and the runnable demo</summary>
<p>
        <code class="inline language-java">requestedAt</code> records when the individual attempt begins,
        <code class="inline language-java">readyAt</code> when it is ready for execution admission,
        <code class="inline language-java">startedAt</code> when execution begins, before destination-specific preparation, and
        <code class="inline language-java">completedAt</code> when the terminal result exists. These wall-clock values are useful for correlation.
        For elapsed time, use <a href="/analyzing-send-results.html#section-send-timings"><code>outcome.getDiagnostics()</code></a>, collected automatically
        with the observer. Its monotonic scheduling measurement includes admission and worker waiting, not just time in a queue.
        A preparation failure reached during execution has ready and started times. A scheduling failure has a ready time but no started time.
        Unavailable ready/start values return <code>null</code>. The application metrics adapter above must treat a null duration as unmeasured,
        while still recording the outcome; it must not record it as a zero-duration sample.
      </p>
      <p>
        <code class="inline language-java">isLoggingOnly()</code> reports the Mailer's configured mode, not server acceptance. A successful logging-only
        attempt still has an <code class="inline language-java">UNKNOWN</code> submission receipt because no SMTP server was contacted. Message-ID getters
        return only real initial or effective Message-ID values; missing IDs remain <code class="inline language-java">null</code> rather than receiving a
        synthetic correlation value.
      </p>
      <p>
        The manually runnable
        <a href="https://github.com/bbottema/simple-java-mail/blob/codex/10.0.0/modules/simple-java-mail/src/test/java/demo/MailSendObserverDemoApp.java">MailSendObserverDemoApp</a>
        sends several emails asynchronously and prints each terminal outcome immediately with its Message-ID, individual measurements and total time, completion count,
        status, and callback thread. Enable <code class="inline language-java">DemoAppBase</code> logging-only mode to try the stream without an SMTP server.
      </p>
</details>

    </div>
  </section>

  <section><div class="wide full-width-example">
    <h3>Choose where the observer runs</h3>
    <p>Increment a local metric inline. The callback finishes before the send returns or its async completion resolves.
      For example, register the metrics callback above with one argument.</p>
<pre><code class="language-java">mailerBuilder.withSendHandlers(MailSendHandlers.builder()
    .withResultObserver(new MetricsMailSendObserver())
    .build());</code></pre>

    <p>If the callback publishes an audit event to a broker, pass an executor. Simple Java Mail attempts one handoff before the send completes.
      Track the broker acknowledgement separately.</p>
<pre><code class="language-java">ExecutorService observationWorkers = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(2);
Mailer mailer = mail.mailerBuilder()
    .withSendHandlers(MailSendHandlers.builder().withResultObserver(outcome -&gt; auditQueue.publish(outcome), observationWorkers).build())
    .buildMailer();

// Sends may complete before publish() runs. A broker acknowledgement
// is separate again from processing that event on another system.
MailSend&lt;MailSubmissionReceipt&gt; send = mailer.async().sendMail(email);
send.getCompletion().join();

mailer.close();              // finishes SJM work and handoff attempts
observationWorkers.shutdown(); // application owns this executor
// Await its termination if this application needs all callbacks to finish.
// Await downstream acknowledgements separately if your workflow needs them.</code></pre>
    <p>Your application owns the supplied executor, its queue and shutdown, and the audit event’s persistence and delivery.</p>
<details class="doc-disclosure">
  <summary>Callback threads, rejection and executor policies</summary>
<p>Either overload replaces the whole registration; the one-argument overload restores inline dispatch.
      These methods belong to <code>MailSendHandlers.builder()</code>; install the immutable group with <code>withSendHandlers(...)</code>.
      These are runtime Java integrations, not properties or CLI values, and they are not exposed through OperationalConfig.</p>
    <p>Default callback threads are the calling thread for synchronous sends and preparation/admission failures, the send worker for running async
      work, and a private Mailer completion worker for cancelled/expired work that never started. Different sends can notify concurrently.
      A slow inline callback or completion continuation can delay other terminal notifications on that private worker, but not their deadline signals.</p>
    <p>The executor overload uses the application executor's actual policy. A direct executor or CallerRunsPolicy can still execute inline;
      a blocking execute() can delay handoff. Rejected handoffs are logged as undelivered notifications, without retry or inline fallback.
      A silent-discard executor cannot report a lost notification. Callback RuntimeExceptions are logged and ignored in both modes.</p>
<p>Outcomes are frozen before either callback execution or handoff.
      For updates during a send, use the separate <a href="/analyzing-send-results.html#section-live-progress">live-progress observer</a>.
      Its dispatcher keeps callbacks off send threads even with an inline executor. Updates can be coalesced or dropped.</p>    <p>The manual <a href="https://github.com/bbottema/simple-java-mail/blob/codex/10.0.0/modules/simple-java-mail/src/test/java/demo/MailSendExecutionDemoApp.java">MailSendExecutionDemoApp</a>
      requests cancellation and uses an executor-backed observer. It awaits send completion and observer aggregation separately.</p>
</details>

  </div></section>
  <section><div class="wide">
    <h3>Choose handlers for each send</h3>
    <p>Use <code>withSendHandlersFactory(sendId -&gt; ...)</code> when each send needs its own application context or callbacks.
      Simple Java Mail generates one UUID and calls the factory once per logical email, before preparation and individual-send admission.
      Retries and failover retain that identity and those handlers. Sending the same Email twice creates two separate identities.</p>
    <pre><code class="language-java">mailerBuilder.withSendHandlersFactory(sendId -&gt; MailSendHandlers.builder()
    .withProgressObserver(progress -&gt; dashboard.update(sendId, progress))
    .withResultObserver(outcome -&gt; audit.record(sendId, outcome))
    .build());</code></pre>
    <p>Fixed handlers and factory-produced handlers both receive notifications, fixed first, with callback failures isolated independently.
      For a single send the factory runs on the caller thread; lazy batches invoke it only for each reached Email on the executing thread.
      Rehearsals and connection tests do not invoke it. A factory exception or null result stops that email before submission; fixed handlers
      and enabled telemetry can still report a factory failure once an ID exists.</p>
    <p><code>withSendIdGenerator(...)</code> replaces the UUID generator when your application owns send identities. It must be thread-safe and
      return unique, non-null IDs. Generation failure stops the send without an observation ID. Keep the default when no custom identity is needed.</p>
    <p>Use a group's <code>toBuilder()</code> to edit result or progress registration independently, then supply the rebuilt group.
      <code>clearSendHandlers()</code> removes fixed registrations; <code>clearSendHandlersFactory()</code> removes the per-send factory;
      <code>resetSendIdGenerator()</code> restores random UUIDs. Existing Mailers and borrowed executors remain unchanged.</p>
  </div></section>
  <a href="#section-correlate-application-jobs" id="section-correlate-application-jobs" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
  <h3>Correlate sends with application jobs</h3>
  <section>
    <div class="wide">
      <p>One shared Mailer can send invoice-1001 and invoice-1002 while keeping each job's progress and full outcome together,
        even when both calls reuse the same Email. Supply a handler factory that captures the current job ID on the submitting thread.
        Its callbacks retain that value after the submission call returns.</p>
      <p>The application-owned
        <a href="/assets/guides/job-correlation/JobCorrelatedMailer.java">JobCorrelatedMailer helper</a>
        wraps individual asynchronous sends. It sets a private ThreadLocal only around <code>mailer.async().sendMail(email)</code>.
        Inside the factory it copies the job ID into a final local value; the progress and outcome closures use that capture.
        A <code>finally</code> block restores any previous value, or removes the entry, immediately after initiating the send.
        The callbacks never read the ThreadLocal later.</p>
      <p>These three identities have different jobs:</p>
      <table><thead><tr><th>Identity</th><th>Example</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody>
        <tr><td>Application job ID</td><td><code>invoice-1001</code></td><td>Your business record, captured by the application helper.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Observation UUID</td><td>A separate generated UUID for each call</td><td>Correlates this logical send's progress, diagnostics and terminal outcome. Retries and failover retain it and the same captured handlers.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>MIME Message-ID</td><td><code>&lt;invoice-notice@example.org&gt;</code></td><td>Message content metadata. The reused Email below deliberately has the same Message-ID in both sends.</td></tr>
      </tbody></table>
      <p>The <a href="/assets/guides/job-correlation/JobCorrelationExample.java">complete example with imports</a>
        creates its callbacks and application-owned observer executor explicitly. Its method accepts an already approved SMTP builder;
        it runs only when your application calls it. Both submissions use the same helper and its shared Mailer:</p>
<pre><code class="language-java small">public static void sendTwoInvoices(SimpleJavaMail mail, MailerRegularBuilder&lt;?&gt; approvedSmtpBuilder)
        throws Exception {
    Email sameEmail = mail.emailBuilder().startingBlank()
            .from("invoices@example.org")
            .to("customer@example.org")
            .withSubject("Your invoice is available")
            .withPlainText("Sign in to view your invoices.")
            .fixingMessageId("&lt;invoice-notice@example.org&gt;")
            .buildEmail();

    ExecutorService observationWorkers = Executors.newSingleThreadExecutor();
    JobCorrelatedMailer.JobEvents events = new JobCorrelatedMailer.JobEvents() {
        @Override
        public void onProgress(String jobId, UUID observationId, MailSendProgress progress) {
            System.out.printf("%s / %s: %s%n", jobId, observationId, progress.getStage());
        }

        @Override
        public void onOutcome(String jobId, UUID observationId, MailSendOutcome outcome) {
            System.out.printf("%s / %s: successful=%s%n", jobId, observationId, outcome.isSuccessful());
        }
    };

    try (JobCorrelatedMailer jobs = new JobCorrelatedMailer(approvedSmtpBuilder, events, observationWorkers)) {
        MailSend&lt;MailSubmissionReceipt&gt; first = jobs.sendAsync("invoice-1001", sameEmail);
        MailSend&lt;MailSubmissionReceipt&gt; second = jobs.sendAsync("invoice-1002", sameEmail);
        first.getCompletion().join();
        second.getCompletion().join();
    } finally {
        observationWorkers.shutdown();
        if (!observationWorkers.awaitTermination(30, TimeUnit.SECONDS)) {
            throw new IllegalStateException("Outcome callbacks have not drained");
        }
    }
}</code></pre>
      <p>Individual async sends invoke the handler factory on their caller thread, so the helper captures each job before that scope ends.
        Lazy batches initialize their per-email handlers on the execution thread; a caller's ThreadLocal does not automatically travel there.
        Use this wrapper for individual sends only. The application must choose a separate propagation approach for a lazy batch.</p>
      <p>Progress is lossy: updates can be combined or dropped and can arrive after the terminal outcome.
        Use the full outcome and any receipt to decide what happened, and do not require every progress stage to arrive.
        Reusing a business job ID in another call still creates another observation UUID.</p>
      <p>The executor belongs to the application. Closing the helper drains its Mailer's send work and observer handoff attempts;
        it does not drain executor-backed callbacks or make their storage durable. This example waits for its callback executor separately.
        Durable job/result storage, rejection handling and downstream acknowledgements remain application responsibilities.</p>
      <p>The accompanying <a href="/assets/guides/job-correlation/README.md">integration and verification notes</a> explain the helper's scope.
        Its executable fixture uses a CustomMailer without SMTP and checks two concurrent sends, two queued sends, a capacity rejection,
        captured job/outcome identities and immediate context cleanup. It deliberately makes no promise about receiving every progress event.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <a href="#section-mailer-lifecycle" id="section-mailer-lifecycle" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  {{#> components/feature-heading-h2 batch=true spring=true}}Mailer lifecycle and resource ownership{{/components/feature-heading-h2}}
  <section><div class="wide">
    <p>Keep one Mailer for the lifetime of your job or service. For a job that queues two reports, keep the completions so you can see whether
      the sends succeeded. Let try-with-resources close the Mailer when the job finishes.</p>
<pre><code class="language-java small">try (Mailer mailer = mailerBuilder.buildMailer()) {
    CompletableFuture&lt;MailSubmissionReceipt&gt; first =
            mailer.async().sendMail(firstEmail).getCompletion();
    CompletableFuture&lt;MailSubmissionReceipt&gt; second =
            mailer.async().sendMail(secondEmail).getCompletion();

    CompletableFuture.allOf(first, second)
            .join();
}</code></pre>
    <p><code>close()</code> stops new sends, drains accepted work and releases owned resources. Wait from the thread that owns the Mailer.
      An inline observer or send callback can start cleanup with <code>closeAsync()</code> and return so the send can finish.</p>
    <h3>Who closes what?</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Direct or pooled Mailer.</strong> Close each Mailer you build. It drains accepted sends before closing its pool registration
        and shuts down its built-in executor. A synchronous direct call closes its own transport. The default executor starts workers only for async work.</li>
      <li><strong>Shared executor.</strong> Leave your executor running while the Mailer drains. Its owner shuts it down when the other users are finished.</li>
      <li><strong>Spring bean.</strong> Spring closes the managed Mailer when the context stops. Close any Mailers you create outside bean management yourself.</li>
    </ul>
<details class="doc-disclosure">
  <summary>Waiting for cleanup and handling close failures</summary>
<p><code>closeAsync()</code> returns a <code>CompletableFuture&lt;Void&gt;</code> for the same cleanup, including without a pool.
      Repeated calls give independent views. Cancelling or completing one view leaves cleanup running. Slow I/O and callbacks can delay shutdown.
      Each accepted send keeps its deadline and its own result.</p>
      <p>Blocking <code>close()</code> declares <code>ExecutionException</code> for cleanup failures (inspect its cause), and <code>InterruptedException</code>
      if the caller stops waiting. After interruption, use <code>closeAsync()</code> to observe completion.</p><pre><code class="language-java small" id="example-mailer-close-exceptions">void runMailWork(MailerRegularBuilder&lt;?&gt; configuredBuilder, Iterable&lt;Email&gt; emails)
        throws ExecutionException, InterruptedException {
    try (Mailer mailer = configuredBuilder.buildMailer()) {
        for (Email email : emails) {
            mailer.sync().sendMail(email);
        }
    }
}</code></pre>
</details>
<details class="doc-disclosure">
  <summary>Observers, previews and write-timeout workers</summary>
<ul>
      <li>Shutdown drains accepted work on supplied executors and the Mailer’s private completion worker, and waits for result-observer handoffs.
        Track executor-backed callbacks and downstream work in your application. Pending progress updates are dropped after draining and running progress callbacks can finish.</li>
      <li>Connection tests and probes stop opening connections when shutdown starts. Queued async tests check again before opening one. Use a new Mailer for later connections.
        <a href="/debugging.html#rehearsal-after-close">Standalone rehearsal and validation</a> use the retained preparation settings even during or after close,
        independently of a later Mailer reusing the Session. Work inside an accepted send or on the Mailer’s own executor follows the enclosing shutdown and blocking-close rules.</li>
      <li>Connections from a managed Angus Session share their write-timeout monitor until the last connection closes. Returning a connection to the pool keeps the monitor available.
        Your application closes any scheduler it supplied. See <a href="configuration.html#socket-timeouts">socket timeouts</a>.</li></ul>
</details>
  </div></section>

  <a href="#section-mail-send-retries" id="section-mail-send-retries" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Retry a temporary refusal without holding a worker</h2>
  <section><p class="wide">Optional <a href="/mail-send-retries.html">mail-send retries</a> keep one completion and one final observer outcome.
    Ordinary asynchronous retry waits release their worker, lease and proxy, then resume on the configured executor. Each actual invocation consumes
    the selected server's sending allowance. A partial or uncertain result is never automatically replayed. Simple batches and open-connection callbacks
    deliberately retain their connection and thread during waits; graceful shutdown drains accepted retries before closing owned execution resources.</p></section>
